## Problem

The `formatTypeString` and `formatPrimitiveType` functions in `lib/schema.ts` include the OpenAPI `format` field in the rendered type display for all types. This produces output like `integer (int32)`, `integer (int64)`, and `number (double)` in property tables.

These numeric formats are implementation details (reflecting Go's `int32`/`int64`/`float64` types) and aren't meaningful to API callers — JSON has a single number type, so the distinction is invisible on the wire.

Meanwhile, formats like `date-time` and `uuid` **are** meaningful and should continue to be displayed.

## Proposal

Filter displayed formats to only include semantically meaningful ones. Something like:

```typescript
const semanticFormats = new Set(["date-time", "uuid", "uri", "email"]);

function formatTypeString(schema: Schema): string {
  let type = schema.type || "unknown";

  if (schema.format && semanticFormats.has(schema.format)) {
    type = `${type} (${schema.format})`;
  }
  if (schema.nullable) {
    type = `${type}|null`;
  }

  return type;
}
```

The same change applies to `formatPrimitiveType`.

## Before / After

| Before | After |
|--------|-------|
| `integer (int32)` | `integer` |
| `integer (int64)` | `integer` |
| `number (double)` | `number` |
| `number (float)` | `number` |
| `string (date-time)` | `string (date-time)` — unchanged |
| `string (uuid)` | `string (uuid)` — unchanged |
| `integer (int64)\|null` | `integer\|null` |

The OpenAPI spec YAML/JSON output is not affected — only the human-readable rendering.
